The most common two-release edit: a column every row should have. Release
2 adds `slug`, filled @from the name; it may still hold NULL, for the
rows the app writes before it sets one. Release 3 makes it NOT NULL: the
rows are copied, and every one has a slug. (One release could not do both:
an @from fills only a column new to its table.)

== before
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;
INSERT INTO dish VALUES (1, 'Pea Soup'), (2, 'Rye Bread');
PRAGMA user_version = 1;

== after
-- @release 2
-- @from 2 column dish slug = lower(replace(name, ' ', '-'))
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, slug TEXT) STRICT;

== after
-- @release 3
-- @from 2 column dish slug = lower(replace(name, ' ', '-'))
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, slug TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE) STRICT;

== plan
-- after 1
roux: migrating test.db, 4 steps
  table `dish`: aside, to make it anew
    ALTER TABLE "dish" RENAME TO "roux_old_dish"
  table `dish`: as schema.sql makes it
    CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, slug TEXT) STRICT
  table `dish`: its rows, by id, name, slug = lower(replace(name, ' ', '-'))
    INSERT INTO "dish" ("id", "name", "slug") SELECT "id", "name", (
    lower(replace(name, ' ', '-'))
    ) FROM "roux_old_dish"
  table `dish`: the old one
    DROP TABLE "roux_old_dish"
migrated from release 1
-- after 2
roux: migrating test.db, 4 steps
  table `dish`: aside, to make it anew
